    18	## [1.4.0] - 2026-09-07
    19	
    20	Continuity, delivery and installer-safety train (PLAN-179 compaction
    21	continuity and work-boundary persistence; PLAN-182 per-project audit
    22	isolation; PLAN-183 delivery routes and an upgrade that finally
    23	delivers `docs/` and `.github/`; PLAN-185 installer write
    24	confinement; PLAN-169 a `user` profile derived from the base instead
    25	of hand-maintained). The headline for an adopter is that
    26	`upgrade.sh` now delivers the two trees it silently skipped for four
    27	releases, and that every delivered-template destination is now confined
    28	to the directory you hand it (the seams that are not — backups, the
    29	schema-doc refresh, `.gitignore` appends, the dispatcher copies, the
    30	`state/` seed — are signed conditions of this rc, with the pre-upgrade
    31	checks in `docs/UPGRADE-PROCEDURE.md`; the cross-model re-pass verdicts committed
    32	with the release evidence are an annex of this pre-release — every P1 they list
    33	is a mandatory cure before the GA). Two security fixes in the install path, one
    34	audit-log scope change adopters share a machine over, and the honest
    35	part: the compaction-continuity feature this train is named after
    36	shipped only after its first design was measured and found to deliver
    37	nothing. As always: governance and auditability — no speed claim.
    38	
    39	### Fixed — `upgrade.sh` silently skipped `docs/` and `.github/` (PLAN-183, ADR-194)
    40	
    41	The most consequential adopter-visible defect closed in this release.
    42	From v1.0.0 through v1.3.0, `scripts/upgrade.sh` never delivered the
    43	`docs/` and `.github/` trees that `install.sh` delivers — an adopter
    44	who installed once and upgraded thereafter kept the *original*
    45	`docs/BRANCH-PROTECTION.md`, `docs/rotation-log.md`, `.github/CODEOWNERS`
    46	and the two CI workflow templates forever, with no warning.
    47	
    48	- **Upgrade now delivers both trees**, hash-gated against the git
    49	  generations of the SOURCE file: a byte-pristine copy of a known prior
    50	  framework generation is replaced, anything you edited is PRESERVED
    51	  loudly. A pre-existing copy of yours that is byte-identical to ANY
    52	  generation, the current one included, counts as the framework's own
    53	  copy too — replaced or mode-normalised, and registered as
    54	  framework-owned — even if the framework never delivered it (signed
    55	  condition of rc.1: edit or move such a file before upgrading).
    56	  Backups go to `.claude.bak/<timestamp>` without the destination
    57	  confinement the deliveries get: keep that path absent or empty, never
    58	  a symlink (signed condition of rc.1). Delivery is registered
    59	  only after it actually happened, when a prior registration's digest
    60	  still matches, or — the third route — when a pre-existing file's bytes
    61	  equal the current or a historical generation of the source (naked byte
    62	  equality; signed conditions 5 and 42). A delivery that
    63	  fails its PRECONDITION (route table, a table row whose source or
    64	  destination relpath is not confined, transform) exits 3 and
    65	  persists `upgrade_succeeded: false` in the install-state rather than
    66	  recording a full upgrade that did not happen (`6304f66`); a failure of
    67	  the WRITER or the RENDERER after a route was selected (a read-only
    68	  `docs/`, a failed tempfile or rename, a failed CODEOWNERS render) is
    69	  still reported PRESERVED with exit 0 — signed condition of rc.1: read
    70	  the delivery summary, a PRESERVED route you never edited is a failed
    71	  delivery. A sensitive path already TRACKED by git
    72	  (`.claude/settings.local.json`, `.claude/state`,
    73	  `state/mcp_client_secrets`) aborts the upgrade AFTER hooks, scripts and
    74	  settings were rewritten (signed condition of rc.1: `git ls-files` over
    75	  those paths must print nothing before upgrading).
    76	  A route whose SOURCE file is missing (or not a regular file) in the
    77	  executing checkout is counted SKIPPED, not failed — the run still exits
    78	  0 (signed condition of rc.1: upgrade from a complete checkout and read
    79	  the delivery summary; any SKIPPED route without `--pin` means an
    80	  incomplete upgrade).
    81	  A route whose transform has no renderer in the running version is
    82	  a failed delivery too: named, `upgrade_succeeded: false`, exit 3
    83	  (`5518888`, all four sites of the class).
    84	- **One route table, three readers.** `scripts/delivery-routes.tsv` is
    85	  now the single answer to "which source file produces this
    86	  destination?", read by the manifest generator, `doctor.sh` and the
    87	  parity classifier — no reader carries a private copy. Four separate
    88	  defects (`upgrade.sh` not delivering, the parity classifier and the
    89	  manifest generator resolving the wrong source, and `doctor.sh`
    90	  REPAIRING with the wrong file) all had the same shape: no single
    91	  owner for that question. Contract: ADR-194 (`b6de7cf`, `aaf32c7`,
    92	  `6304f66`, `3bc3638`).
    93	- **`doctor.sh` repaired deliveries from the wrong source** before this
    94	  release; the positive control reproduced a leak of the maintainer's
    95	  live `.github/CODEOWNERS` into an adopter repo. `doctor.sh` now reads
    96	  the same route table (`6304f66`).
    97	- **Shallow-clone caveat, named:** in CI with `actions/checkout` at
    98	  depth 1 the hash gate cannot see older generations, so files are
    99	  PRESERVED (safe) and reported `STALE`. Deepen the history before
   100	  upgrading in CI if you want the refresh.
   101	
   102	### Security — delivered-template destinations are confined to the target (PLAN-185, ADR-196)
   103	
   104	- **`install.sh` wrote outside `$TARGET`** when a destination path was a
   105	  pending symlink or hardlink. One destination-confinement predicate now
   106	  lives in `scripts/_framework_manifest_set.sh`; `install.sh` pre-flies
   107	  every delivered-template destination before the first write and
   108	  refuses by name, `upgrade.sh` consumes the same predicate. E2E in
   109	  bytes: 105/0 after the cure against 22/33 before it (`cc00235`).
   110	  Several seams stay outside that preflight in this release candidate
   111	  and are signed conditions of rc.1: the `state/mcp_client_secrets`
   112	  seed (a symlinked `TARGET/state` is followed by `mkdir -p`/`chmod`);
   113	  a refused SOURCE template (symlink outside the checkout) that install
   114	  reports as `SKIP` and upgrade as `PRESERVED` while still exiting 0; a
   115	  source MISSING from the executing checkout (`SKIP`, exit 0, on both
   116	  install and upgrade); the `.gitignore`/`.claude/.gitignore` appends,
   117	  which test `-L` only, so a hard link there is written through; the
   118	  `.claude/dispatcher/` copies, which run no check at all (a symlink or a
   119	  hard link there is written through); and `install_dispatcher`, which
   120	  re-copies unconditionally on a rerun (edited dispatcher files are
   121	  overwritten — back them up first); and the deny-baseline merge of
   122	  `install.sh`, which writes a PID-named sibling of `.claude/settings.json`
   123	  outside the preflight (a pre-placed symlink there is written through —
   124	  the `find` check over `.claude/` in `docs/UPGRADE-PROCEDURE.md` catches
   125	  it; run it before a fresh install too).
   126	- **`--github-owner` with a `/` in it left `.github/CODEOWNERS` at zero
   127	  bytes, permanently.** The handle grammar is now shared by producer and
   128	  consumer, the file is rendered through a pipe and written atomically,
   129	  and a truncated CODEOWNERS is recovered from delivery EVIDENCE
   130	  (`cc00235`) — evidence meaning a manifest line that NAMES the path;
   131	  the line's digest grammar is not validated, so keep the manifest
   132	  intact; a CODEOWNERS you switched off survives only a re-run WITHOUT
   133	  `--github-owner` — deleting the file makes the installer render it
   134	  again, and an emptied file with a manifest record is re-rendered too
   135	  (signed condition 61 of rc.1).
   136	- **`uninstall.sh` followed a crafted manifest out of the target.** The
   137	  install manifest is a plain file in your repo and is NOT
   138	  integrity-checked before the removal walk; pre-cure, a record naming
   139	  `../outside/victim.txt` was REMOVED, and with a symlinked `docs`
   140	  component the walk deleted an outside file and the backup archived
   141	  bytes read through the link. Every removal, every backup entry and
   142	  every restore member OUTSIDE `.claude/` is now tested lexically
   143	  (absolute paths, `..` segments, control characters, whitespace and glob
   144	  metacharacters, option-like `-` leaders) and physically (a symlinked
   145	  ancestor under the target) (`6160578`); the `.claude` subtree of a
   146	  restore archive is still extracted whole after a name-only check
   147	  (signed condition 57 of rc.1: restore only unmodified archives this
   148	  uninstaller produced).
   149	- **`doctor.sh` stopped discarding unsafe manifest records in silence** —
   150	  traversal, absolute path, any control byte, symlinked ancestor,
   151	  malformed digest and duplicate relpath are each a NAMED discard, capped
   152	  at 20 and folded into `UNRESOLVED` (rc 1); a manifest containing a NUL
   153	  is refused before the parse (`ba15c71`).
   154	- A fail-closed census of installer write sites
   155	  (`check-installer-write-safety.py`) ships as a RATCHET in `validate.yml`
   156	  with its blind spots declared in the plan, not papered over.
   157	
   158	### Changed — audit log resolves per PROJECT, not per `$HOME` (PLAN-182, ADR-001)
   159	
   160	**Adopter-visible if you run the framework in more than one repository
   161	under the same `$HOME`.** Runtime state — the HMAC audit log, its key,
   162	lock, errors, salt and sidecars — now resolves through one family
   163	resolver (`.claude/hooks/_lib/runtime_paths.py`) using Claude Code's own
   164	path-based project slug, instead of a literal `ceo-orchestration`
   165	directory shared by every project (`9de4efc`, `965fb13`, `3d16070`).
   166	
   167	- For repositories with DISTINCT resolved runtime directories, this separates
   168	  their chains and keys and provides correct
   169	  `project` attribution for the emitters that carry the field (a handful
   170	  of newly specified actions still omit `project` or `session_id` and
   171	  cannot be attributed in a shared `CEO_AUDIT_LOG_PATH` — rc.1 condition
   172	  23), a `verify_chain()` that means something per project — for stretches
   173	  written by one writer at a time: the previous HMAC is still read before
   174	  the log lock is taken, so two parallel writers can chain to the same
   175	  predecessor and a break is then reported that nobody caused (signed
   176	  condition 67 of rc.1; same order as v1.3.0) — and a per-project HMAC key
   177	  and salt, preventing correlation by `prompt_sha256` between those
   178	  repositories. The path-derived slug can collide: `/srv/a-b/c` and
   179	  `/srv/a/b-c` both yield `-srv-a-b-c`. Verify distinct resolved directories
   180	  before using multiple repositories (rc.1 condition 30); a collision shares
   181	  the log, key and salt. The per-project key is minted on first use and that first
   182	  mint is not exclusive: create it with a single writer before the first
   183	  session (signed condition 68 of rc.1; `docs/UPGRADE-PROCEDURE.md`,
   184	  check 11).
   185	- **The old location is not migrated for you.** The pre-v1.4.0 chain
   186	  under `$HOME/.claude/projects/ceo-orchestration/` stays where it is;
   187	  `SPEC/v1/audit-log.schema.md` records the change as v2.58 and names the
   188	  legacy path.
   189	- **Plan state and scratchpads move too, and are not migrated either.**
   190	  `state_store.py` resolves through the same family resolver, so a
   191	  repository with an ACTIVE plan on v1.3.0 opens a NEW, empty state
   192	  store after the upgrade — inter-agent handoffs report missing state,
   193	  `/resume` finds no cached graph, while the old SQLite files stay on disk under the
   194	  legacy directory. Two documented routes: set `CEO_PROJECT_NAME` to
   195	  the legacy slug (the explicit escape hatch in `state_store.py`) until
   196	  the plan closes, or copy ONLY `scratchpad/`, `skill_proposals/`,
   197	  `skill_index/` and `session_graph/` from `<legacy>/state/` into
   198	  `<new-runtime-root>/state/`, plus the root-level `session-graphs/` (the
   199	  cached `/resume` graphs; `/resume` otherwise rebuilds from the audit log,
   200	  git and the plan markdown and never reads the stores — sessions recorded
   201	  before the upgrade stay in the legacy chain). The CLI
   202	  `python3 .claude/hooks/_lib/runtime_paths.py --state-dir` prints
   203	  `<new-runtime-root>`; append `/state` for the store destination.
   204	  NEVER copy the whole legacy `state/` directory or its flat files:
   205	  its audit recovery spools can contaminate the new project's chain.
   206	  First drain or quarantine those spools in the legacy context; follow
   207	  `docs/UPGRADE-PROCEDURE.md` and rc.1 condition 27. Automatic migration
   208	  remains a pre-GA follow-up.
   209	- **The limit that does NOT go away:** under the same UID one project's
   210	  process can still read another's `0700` directory and `0600` key. A
   211	  real boundary needs a separate UID; that is out of scope by decision,
   212	  and said here rather than implied away.
   213	- The resolver ships with a CLI
   214	  (`python3 .claude/hooks/_lib/runtime_paths.py --state-dir|--slug|--project-dir
   215	  [--project PATH]`) used by the pre-push templates and by
   216	  `ceo-backup`/`ceo-restore` (`3d16070`).
