   665	## [1.2.0] - 2026-07-30
   666	
   667	Substrate + rail release (PLAN-160/161/163/164). The headline is not a new
   668	feature: it is that the cross-model pair-rail **completed a live in-hook
   669	review for the first time in the audit log's history**. Everything else is
   670	the substrate work that made that possible. As always: governance and
   671	auditability — no speed claim.
   672	
   673	### Fixed — the pair-rail was 100% fail-open (PLAN-164, ADR-110-AMEND-1)
   674	
   675	- **`CEO_PAIR_RAIL_TIMEOUT_S` internal default 30 → 120 s**, and the
   676	  `check_pair_rail.py` PreToolUse **registration timeout 60 → 150 s** in both
   677	  kernel `.claude/settings.json` and `templates/settings/settings.base.json`
   678	  (parity enforced). The 30 s value was an implementation literal, never a
   679	  decided one, and it sat structurally *below* the latency of a real Codex
   680	  verdict: **every one of the 12 `pair_rail_case` events in the entire life of
   681	  the audit log was case F / TIMEOUT.** The rail had never once completed a
   682	  live review. Measured calibration (N=9, same machine): p95 ≈ 75 s, which
   683	  crosses the measurement protocol's own 70 s escalation threshold and selects
   684	  120/150 rather than the 100/120 first draft.
   685	- **The layering invariant is now tested, not assumed**
   686	  (`test_pair_rail_timeout_invariant.py`): kernel registration == template
   687	  registration, and `registration >= internal + 30`. A unilateral flip of any
   688	  of the three literals now goes red in the suite and in the pack preflight.
   689	- **`statusMessage` on the registration** — a session held by a synchronous
   690	  cross-model review shows "may take up to ~3 min" instead of appearing
   691	  frozen. (Shipped in 1.2.0 as "may take 1-2 min"; the wording tracks the
   692	  budget and was retuned by ADR-110-AMEND-2 when it moved to 180/210 s.)
   693	- **From zero completed reviews to ten.** The log now holds 10 healthy cases
   694	  (7 × case A, 3 × case B) alongside 14 case F. Median verdict latency 70.5 s;
   695	  observed maximum 120.0 s.
   696	- **The §3 recalibration trigger (≥10 healthy cases) is met, and it points
   697	  upward — with the caveat it deserves.** No sample actually exceeded 120 s;
   698	  the p95 figure of 122.2 s is an *interpolation above the observed maximum*
   699	  on exactly ten points, not a measured latency. What is solid: the three
   700	  slowest healthy reviews (115 / 115 / 120 s) leave 0–5 s of headroom, and the
   701	  trigger's own query is **right-censored by construction** — any review slower
   702	  than the budget becomes a case F and never enters the healthy set, so this
   703	  p95 can only ever under-report the true distribution. An upward
   704	  `ADR-110-AMEND-2` is therefore indicated, but the new pair is deliberately
   705	  **not** chosen here: §3 requires a new amendment via ceremony, and the number
   706	  belongs to the C5 measurement protocol plus a debate, not to a changelog
   707	  line.
   708	
   709	### Changed — substrate uplift to Claude Code 2.1.220 + Claude 5 (PLAN-163, ADR-181)
   710	
   711	- Model registry refreshed to the **Claude 5 family**. Adopter installs are
   712	  migrated in place by the landed `scripts/upgrade.sh` — including the
   713	  pair-rail **registration-timeout cap 60 → 150 s** (the settings half of
   714	  ADR-110-AMEND-1; applied only when the adopter's value is still the old
   715	  default, so an operator-chosen value is never clobbered).
   716	- **Dated pricing is now event-date-aware** rather than resolved against wall
   717	  clock, so a historical audit event prices at the rate in force when it
   718	  happened.
   719	- `opus-4-8-fast` recognized; stale-model scan updated.
